A||N||3||mfleury||88 GT Headlight||||1||
Z||000000||mfleury||02-19-2001||12:48 PM||||Can anyone help on this..!!!<BR>On my 88 GT the headlights goes up as you turn it on but will not go down, I check all cables and still could not find the problem. Does any one knows what could be wrong??<BR>Thanks..<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||02-19-2001||02:51 PM||||My guess would be headlight switch faulty, or a break in the "headlights down" (white) wire to the controller.  The white wire from headlight switch should get +12v when headlight switch is turned off.<P>Blown taillight fuse could cause the "headlights won't go down" problem, too.  So check to see if the taillights work, too - it's easy to check.<P>For more details, try Ogre's cave: <A HREF="http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/tunnel6/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre/tunnel6/index.htm</A> <P> <P>    <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 02-19-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||theogre||02-19-2001||07:25 PM||||<A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A><P>Check the fuses Fist....<P>That's one of the few items I don't have all the wiring for. It would have to be the white wire circuit. It could be the controller. <P>Unplug the headlights at the lamp. It prevents battery draw and a big spark in the next step.<P>Take out the headlight switch, raise the headlights then unplug the switch.<P>jumper the white wire to a 12V circuit. I like to use a cheap cord that fits in the lighter socket for this kind of testing. Most people have one around or can get them cheap enough.<P>I recently saw the wiring for this someplace... I may have it on my machine at work. I'll see if I can find it tomorow.<P>The controller basically works or it doesn't. I'd look at the other stuff first. If the controller is dead you can get on off SunBird and other GM cars with the Gen2 light motor system.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 02-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||mfleury||03-04-2001||03:12 PM||||Great.... You know your stuffs...<P>I check the fuses before and all was fine, at least it show ok when using the tool, but I desided to actualy remove one by one and there it was.. a burnt fuse.. it is working fine now...<P>Thank you all,<P>MF<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
